WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.889 --> 00:00:08.870
Hi my name is Ryan Harriss and in 2018 I directed,
produced and co-wrote my first feature film

00:00:08.870 --> 00:00:11.119
called ZOOEY.

00:00:11.119 --> 00:00:16.240
ZOOEY is a low-budget to no-budget feature
about a young boy who over the course of a

00:00:16.240 --> 00:00:21.150
summer escapes from his abusive stepfather
and goes to stay in this kind of janky motel.

00:00:21.150 --> 00:00:25.119
It’s at this motel that he meets a young
girl named Zooey who is traveling through

00:00:25.119 --> 00:00:26.119
with her brother.

00:00:26.119 --> 00:00:32.119
Noah has formed this sort of bond and over
the course of their time together Noah discovers

00:00:32.119 --> 00:00:36.600
a secret about her that changes the course
of their summer entirely.

00:00:36.600 --> 00:00:40.559
ZOOEY started principal photography in 2018
and it took about nine months to shoot the

00:00:40.559 --> 00:00:46.100
entire film due to the fact that we had to
shoot only on weekends because most of the

00:00:46.100 --> 00:00:50.940
crew and most of our people were working 9-to-5
jobs including myself.

00:00:50.940 --> 00:00:55.699
We also had to fly in our main actor from
another state each time when we were shooting.

00:00:55.699 --> 00:00:59.969
So it kind of gave us where we would shoot
for the weekend and then that week I would

00:00:59.969 --> 00:01:04.440
sit there and work and figure out what we
were going to shoot the next following weekend.

00:01:04.440 --> 00:01:08.659
During this process my team and I learned
so much about everything that we did wrong

00:01:08.659 --> 00:01:12.680
and everything that we did right and I wanted
to share with you three ways to understanding

00:01:12.680 --> 00:01:15.970
your crew on a low-budget to no-budget feature
film.

00:01:15.970 --> 00:01:19.180
Number 1) Your crew wants to know.

00:01:19.180 --> 00:01:24.040
On ZOOEY we had about 10-15 people on set
a day and sometimes we had less than that.

00:01:24.040 --> 00:01:25.470
Sometimes we had 4 to 5.

00:01:25.470 --> 00:01:32.000
Sometimes it was just my DP and I going out
with our talent and shooting due to people’s

00:01:32.000 --> 00:01:34.020
availability because we were only shooting
on weekends.

00:01:34.020 --> 00:01:38.040
We had about 4 1st AC’s and 4 2nd AC’s
[a focus puller or first assistant camera]

00:01:38.040 --> 00:01:39.710
that were on sort of a rotation.

00:01:39.710 --> 00:01:41.700
So some people were there on day one through
three.

00:01:41.700 --> 00:01:45.030
Other people were there on day 20, other people
were there mid-way through the shoot.

00:01:45.030 --> 00:01:48.979
I really wanted to make it a point that whoever
was there on that day understood what we were

00:01:48.979 --> 00:01:51.060
trying to achieve that day.

00:01:51.060 --> 00:01:53.719
Not just technically but emotionally.

00:01:53.719 --> 00:01:58.110
Really know that your crew wants to know and
there is no such thing as oversharing or giving

00:01:58.110 --> 00:02:02.130
too much information to your crew because
ultimately they are there to make it better.

00:02:02.130 --> 00:02:08.250
Number 2) is your crew is just as creative
as everybody else on set.

00:02:08.250 --> 00:02:12.310
With ZOOEY in particular we wanted to make
sure that because we were bring in so many

00:02:12.310 --> 00:02:17.480
different people and didn’t have a very
conventional way of shooting a film.

00:02:17.480 --> 00:02:21.239
We wanted to make sure that the people we
brought in weren’t just technically masterful

00:02:21.239 --> 00:02:26.680
at their specific craft but that they were
just fans of movies and they just loved filmmaking

00:02:26.680 --> 00:02:28.350
in general and loved storytelling.

00:02:28.350 --> 00:02:35.230
We wanted to make it a point that everybody
on set had something that they wanted to say.

00:02:35.230 --> 00:02:40.300
A comment about how a shot was or we used
to gather people around the monitors from

00:02:40.300 --> 00:02:44.239
boom ops to camera department to grips.

00:02:44.239 --> 00:02:48.210
We wanted to bring people in to just look
at it because they are going to see things

00:02:48.210 --> 00:02:50.580
that you’re never going to notice.

00:02:50.580 --> 00:02:55.080
Number 3) Listen to your crew, really listen
to them.

00:02:55.080 --> 00:02:59.190
The thing I noticed on ZOOEY that I learned
pretty quickly was it’s kind of amazing

00:02:59.190 --> 00:03:04.570
how your crew that has surrounded you there
is sort of like a fly on the wall.

00:03:04.570 --> 00:03:09.159
They see everything that happens, they see
everything that goes on and things that you

00:03:09.159 --> 00:03:11.780
don’t even notice or floats right over your
head.

00:03:11.780 --> 00:03:18.799
It’s important that if you provide a platform
for them to come safely talk to you that you

00:03:18.799 --> 00:03:22.970
really listen to what they have to say because
they are going to point out little things,

00:03:22.970 --> 00:03:27.709
little nuances, things you might not notice
or they might come up with ideas that you

00:03:27.709 --> 00:03:30.409
realized would have worked so much better.

00:03:30.409 --> 00:03:35.990
So really take your time to listen to your
crew when they come to you and they talk about

00:03:35.990 --> 00:03:39.799
things because it’s really, really important
and it’s crucial because at the end of the

00:03:39.799 --> 00:03:43.650
day filmmaking is a collaborative experience.

00:03:43.650 --> 00:03:48.580
So that’s it on Three Ways of Understanding
Your Crew on A Low-Budget to No-Budget Feature

00:03:48.580 --> 00:03:49.580
Film.

00:03:49.580 --> 00:03:51.510
And if you have time check out ZOOEY on Amazon
Prime this summer.

00:03:51.510 --> 00:03:52.510
Thanks!

00:03:52.510 --> 00:03:52.520
Question: Which one of these tips helps you
the most?

